## Phase 9 addendum — zone partitioning observed on real data / 2026-08-31

Recorded after the Phase 9 checkpoint had already consolidated and cleared this
file, so it sits here as raw material for the Phase 13 checkpoint rather than
being back-edited into INTERVIEW.md. It confirms, on real data, the weakness
INTERVIEW.md §15.4 already states from a constructed test.

### What was observed

Chasing a separate bug (the shift seed's 00:00-06:00 coverage gap, fixed in
`prisma/seed/technicians.ts` and pinned by `tests/shifts.test.ts`), a full
`/solve` run against `zippy_test` produced 1,317 feasible options and **zero
assignments in all three plans**. The same snapshot solved without
partitioning produced three.

Measured, one snapshot, 439 pending jobs, 102 units:

    technicians on shift at 03:20 local:  4  (tech-07, tech-14, tech-21, tech-28)
    of those, crewing a unit:             3  (unit-07@zone-kor, unit-08@zone-ind,
                                              unit-21@zone-whf)
    pending jobs by zone:  as-zone 335, ir-zone 52, rt-zone 52

    zonePartitionThreshold 1_000_000 → partitioned=false  feasible=1317  assigned=3
    zonePartitionThreshold       400 → partitioned=true   feasible=1317  assigned=0

Note the direction of the threshold, because it reads backwards at a glance:
partitioning fires when `optionCount > threshold`, so the LOW threshold is the
partitioned run and the high one is the whole-fleet matrix. 10,536 options were
generated, so 400 partitions and 1,000,000 does not.

### Why it happens, and why it is not a bug

`partitionByZone` matches a job only against units whose `zoneId` equals the
job's. Every pending job in that database sits in a test-fixture zone
(`as-zone`, `ir-zone`, `rt-zone`) and every crewed unit sits in a seeded zone
(`zone-kor`, `zone-ind`, `zone-whf`), so after partitioning no job shares a
matrix with any unit that could serve it. Unpartitioned, the three live units
are matched across zone boundaries and three jobs are served.

This is exactly the approximation `src/engine/solve.ts` documents and
`tests/solve.test.ts` asserts synthetically — a job near (here, across) a zone
boundary loses a better cross-zone option — showing up on real data rather than
in a constructed two-zone fixture. The behaviour is correct for the design.

### What it does and does not say about the design

It does NOT say partitioning is broken. It DOES say the approximation's cost is
not a rounding error at the tail: in a fleet whose crewed units and whose
pending jobs live in disjoint zones, partitioning takes assignments to zero
while a whole-fleet matrix still finds work. Two conditions produced that here,
both of which can occur in production:

- **Thin coverage.** At 03:20 only 4 of 31 technicians were on shift and only 3
  crewed a unit, so the fleet was small enough that losing any cross-zone
  option lost most of the answer. Partitioning is defended as a scale measure,
  but its damage is worst when the fleet is SMALL relative to the zone count —
  the opposite of the case it exists for.
- **Jobs and units in disjoint zones.** Here an artefact of fixture data, but
  the production analogue is real: a zone whose units are all committed while
  its neighbour has spare capacity.

### Open question for Phase 10

The evaluation harness measures how often this costs anything in a realistic
scenario, which is the number that decides whether the threshold is right, or
whether partitioning should be conditioned on fleet size rather than option
count alone. Until then this is a known approximation with one real-data
observation and no measured frequency — recorded here so the harness has a
concrete case to reproduce, not treated as a defect to fix ahead of the
measurement.
